The options that can be passed to the kernel via the commandline are much too
numerous to be listed here. Soe here is just an overview of the options we wrote
to the cmdline file:%1
    - rootflags: options for mounting the root partition - the btrfs subvolume
          is specified here
    - quiet: suppress output when booting
    - bgrt_disable: suppress display of the OEM logo
    - sysrq_always_enable: allows the usage of SysRq emergency key bindings

The SysRq permssion set through the kernel command line allows executing several
low level kernel shortcuts by pressing the associated key bindings all using the
SysRq key. If your keyboard does not have a SysRq key (it most likely has not),
use the Print key instead. The kernel functions that are made accessible can be
specified by the number that is being passed to the sysrq_always_enable
parameter. For a full list of options, see the Arch Wiki or the Linux kernel
documentation on the topic. With our pre-defined value of 224, the following key
binds are made available:
    - Alt+SysRq+e: send SIGTERM to all processes
    - Alt+SysRq+i: send SIGKILL to all processes
    - Alt+SysRq+u: unmount all file systems and re-mount them in read-only mode
    - Alt+SysRq+r: force reboot
